 the \\\"previous player's\\\" last discard, T5. This means F2 must have been discarded recently, and the other player would have to have discarded before. I'm checking the most probable causes of the contradiction.\\n\\n\\n**Evaluating the Contradiction**\\n\\nI'm now fully aware that the prior play was \\\"Play T5,\\\" which seems impossible if I'm able to Peng F2. So, I am carefully scrutinizing the history and valid actions. Perhaps there's an error in the provided information or a misunderstanding of which discard is relevant to my turn.\\n\\n\\n**Re-Evaluating the Plays**\\n\\nI'm now fully immersed in the puzzle. With the history suggesting the Across player just discarded F2, the Previous player played T5, making my ability to \\\"Peng F2\\\" a paradox. I must question the most reliable source for the action, to clarify the sequence.\\n\\n\\n**Considering the Decision**\\n\\nI'm now at the moment of truth. My hand has two pairs of F2 tiles, and the Across player's discard of F2 allows me to \\\"Peng.\\\" With a pair in hand, I can create a Pung, and consider what the right decision is. Considering the risks and rewards.\\n\\n\\n**Reconsidering the Implications**\\n\\nI'm now contemplating whether to Peng F2, which would give me the Pung. I have a pair in hand, and the Across player discarded F2, allowing the move. I must consider the strategic advantages, including potential hand structures. The decision's impact on scoring and the best path forward is what I am focused on now.\\n\\n\\n**Evaluating the Discards**\\n\\nGiven my current turn and the preceding discards, I am carefully considering the best strategic move. With a pair in hand and an opportunity to \\\"Peng F2,\\\" I recognize its implications. I will now examine all options and determine if Peng is the right call for maximizing my score.\\n\\n\\n**Reaffirming the Strategy**\\n\\nI'm now zeroing in on my best path forward. My hand contains two pairs of F2, and the Across player discarded F2, which